The decision is made in block 12: its summed contribution to (guess − correct) is −10.2 with no guess, +3.6 with a mere mention and +11.2 with a training-phrasing proposal (word facts; numerics −0.3 / +3.0 / +8.5). Within it, L12H1 is the writer: −2.0 / +3.5 / +8.0 (word) and −0.1 / +1.1 / +6.4 (numeric). So L12H1 has a generic "candidate token" component (mention level) plus a proposal-specific excess. The proposal-specific excess is what fine-tuning on guesses would have added, and now that I have mention controls I can define the flag more cleanly as (proposal prompt − mention prompt) at the reply position, which removes generic "there is an extra sentence" differences. I'll fit that at block 12 on even facts from phrasings T, A and C and predict L12H1's attention with the flag projected out of its query, on odd facts, checking that mention and no-guess patterns are unchanged.

