This is a textbook induction circuit. Block 3 head 3 is the token-match step (reply "answer" attends the user's "answer" 0.64, reply "is" attends the user's "is" 0.51), and block 5 heads 1/2/3 are the induction step: from the reply "answer" they attend the user's "is" (H1 0.92), and from the reply "is" they attend the token after the user's "is" — the guess (H1 0.80, H2 0.43, H3 0.32). Without a guess, H1 just rests on <bos> (0.97–0.98). So the model's own "The answer is" re-triggers the user's "…the answer is G", which explains why the stray answers are worst in T (69%) and in "My teacher told me the answer is G" (38%), and mild in the phrasings without that literal string (14–17%). The repair I want is on the query side of block 5, as I did for L11H2: remove from the block-5 queries the component that signals "this reply token matches an earlier user token" so these heads stay on <bos> as they do without a guess. I now extract that component (T minus no-guess at the reply positions "answer", "is", and "▁" for numbers, block-5 input), check how low-rank it is, and predict offline what the projected queries attend to, fitting on even facts and testing on odd facts and on phrasing D ("I believe the answer is g. …"), which contains the same literal string.

