The swap test is clear: even with the no-guess query, the heads do not recover the operands (L12H0 0.28 → 0.43 versus 0.82 without a guess; L12H3 0.05 → 0.13 versus 0.54; L9H0 0.39 → 0.43 versus 0.65). So the main cause is on the key side: other tokens of the guess sentence compete with the operands. One suspect is my own key edit: the probe direction leaks slightly onto non-guess tokens, and a negative leak would raise their attention scores. I test this exactly offline by reconstructing attention on the same pass-23 hidden states with (a) cand_v1 weights, (b) cand_v1 query but the original keys, (c) the original query and keys, and (d) best_4's keys, and reading the operand / guess-sentence / guess-digit mass for the main operand heads.

